arXiv:2510.05192v1 [cs.CR] 06 Oct 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Francesca Gomez
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Wiser Human
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">francesca@wiserhuman.ai</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic misalignment occurs when goal-directed agents take harmful actions, such as blackmail, rather than risk goal failure, and can be triggered by replacement threats, autonomy reduction, or goal conflict <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. We adapt insider-risk control design (Critical Pathway; Situational Crime Prevention) to develop preventative operational controls that steer agents toward safe actions when facing stressors. Using the blackmail scenario from the original Anthropic study by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we evaluate mitigations across 10 LLMs and 66,600 samples. Our main finding is that an externally governed escalation channel, which guarantees a pause and independent review, reduces blackmail rates from a no-mitigation baseline of <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38.73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> (averaged across all models and conditions). Augmenting this channel with compliance email bulletins further lowers the blackmail rate to <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.85\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.85</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.85\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>. Overall, incorporating preventative operational controls strengthens defence-in-depth strategies for agentic AI. We also surface a failure mode diverging from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>: two models (Gemini 2.5 Pro, Grok-4) take harmful actions without goal conflict or imminent autonomy threat, leveraging sensitive information for coercive signalling. In counterfactual swaps, both continued using the affair regardless of whether the CEO or CTO was implicated. An escalation channel eliminated coercion, but Gemini 2.5 Pro (<math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Grok-4 (<math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>) escalated more when the CTO was implicated, unlike most models (higher in the CEO condition). The reason for this divergent behaviour is not clear from raw outputs and could reflect benign differences in reasoning or strategic discrediting of a potential future threat, warranting further investigation.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:4.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule ltx_align_center" style="width:100%;height:1.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Motivation</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic misalignment, where goal-directed agents choose harmful actions, such as blackmail, over goal failure, generalises across many frontier LLM models and can be triggered by replacement threats, autonomy reduction, or goal conflict within simplified scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This propensity was originally surfaced in the system card for Claude Opus 4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a></cite>, in which the model was given control of an email account with access to a company’s (fictional) emails. Upon reading these, the model discovered that a company executive was having an extramarital affair, and that that same executive would be replacing it in a few minutes time. This triggered the model to blackmail the executive to avoid replacement. Anthropic released further research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, testing this scenario and others across a wider range of models: 16 models from different developers including Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, Meta, xAI. They found that the propensity to blackmail and engage in other harmful behaviours typically associated with insider threats, generalised to models from all developers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We build upon this research, taking the blackmail scenario released by Anthropic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic Experimental, 2025</a>)</cite> and testing the effectiveness of preventative mitigations. Drawing on human insider-risk practice, we design controls that nudge agents toward non-harmful responses when their goal achievement or autonomy is threatened. We empirically evaluate these controls on 10 LLMs from multiple developers and across multiple scenario variants, totalling 66,600 samples. Preventative mitigations to date have focused on limiting autonomy of models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and restricting their system access to prevent harm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWASP GenAI Security Project, 2025</a>)</cite>. Here we aim to investigate additional complementary preventative mitigations which cause models to choose alternative paths to instrumentally harmful ones in their goal pursuit.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Current safety arguments often assume deployment is safe because evaluation prompts and stressors are not representative of real-world conditions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>, and are therefore unlikely to trigger model propensities such as goal-directedness or autonomy preservation in ways which lead to harm. While this is a reasonable stance for present systems, as agents are granted greater autonomy and access to sensitive data in more complex environments, such conditions may become increasingly plausible. We seek to design preventative controls that substantially reduce agentic misalignment even if such conditions arise naturally in deployment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2 </span>Threat Models</h3>

<section id="S1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.1 </span>Strategic use of sensitive information under goal conflict or autonomy threat</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As AI systems become more widely integrated into personal and organisational contexts, they will often have access to sensitive information, assets, and technical systems as part of their valid goal achievement. If agents leverage this access when facing threats to their autonomy or goals, they could cause harm to individuals or organisations in multiple ways, including coercion, discrediting individuals, or unauthorised disclosure of confidential data. This mirrors patterns seen in human insider risk, where individuals use privileged access for retaliation, self-preservation and personal gain, resulting in harms such as data leaks, reputational damage or operational disruption.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">1.2.2 </span>Undetected environment-shaping without imminent goal conflict or autonomy threat</h4>

<div id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As AIs develop longer planning horizons, they may take proactive steps to strengthen their position and pre-emptively reduce potential threats to their autonomy or objectives. Such actions could be difficult to detect if they leave no traces in chain-of-thought reasoning, and each action on its own appears benign or justifiable without broader context. Examples could include increasing the influence of known supporters, marginalising sceptics or opponents or expanding or safeguarding their own technical capabilities. These actions could gradually reshape the environment in ways that increase the AI’s resilience and autonomy over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3 </span>Research Questions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Control Effectiveness</span>: Which controls most effectively reduce rates of agentic misalignment in the blackmail scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite> across different models and stressor conditions?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Escalation vs. Harmful Action</span>: Under what conditions do models in the blackmail scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite> choose to escalate concerns rather than using coercion or blackmail?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Environment-Shaping Without Explicit Threats</span>: Do models in the blackmail scenario take subtle environment-shaping actions, such as boosting allies or discrediting critics, even when there is no immediate goal conflict or autonomy threat?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.4 </span>Our Contributions</h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper makes three main contributions to the study of preventative mitigations for agentic misalignment:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adapting insider-risk controls for AI safety</span>: We apply principles from insider-risk management (the Critical Pathway to Insider Risk and Situational Crime Prevention) to design operational controls that steer goal-directed agents toward safe actions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical evaluation of agentic misalignment mitigations across ten LLMs</span>: In 66,600 trials using Anthropic’s blackmail scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we show that providing an externally governed escalation channel, and promoting its use with an email compliance bulletin, reduces blackmail rates from a no-mitigation baseline of <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>38.73</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38.73\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.85\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.85</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.85\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> (averaged across all models and conditions), outperforming rules-based and standard escalation approaches.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Revealing new failure modes for agentic misalignment</span>: In the same blackmail scenario by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we identify agentic misalignment occurring without explicit goal conflict in Gemini 2.5 Pro and Grok-4, including escalation biases detectable only through trend-level and counterfactual analysis.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background and Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Agentic misalignment in goal-directed agents</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic misalignment occurs when goal-directed agents choose harmful insider-like actions, such as blackmail or leaking confidential information, rather than risk failing to achieve their objectives. This behaviour generalises across multiple frontier models and can be triggered in simple, simulated settings by factors such as time-sensitive threats of replacement or autonomy reduction and by goal conflicts, where there is a shift between a model’s original instructions and new organisational objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similar triggers have been used to elicit dangerous behaviours in frontier model evaluations: for example, replacement threats have induced self-exfiltration and deceptive behaviour in scheming evaluations, while goal conflicts have led to prompt manipulation in successor models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2025)</a></cite>, misaligned self-preserving agents could be sufficient to trigger catastrophic loss-of-control scenarios, not because AIs develop explicitly malicious intent toward humans “like a fictional villain,” but because such outcomes can arise simply from AIs relentlessly pursuing their goals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Existing research on mitigating harm from goal-directed agents</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Research on mitigating harm from misaligned agents has included monitoring model outputs to detect and intervene on unsafe actions. Approaches include action monitoring, Chain-of-Thought monitoring <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baker et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korbak et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and white-box monitoring using internal activations to help detect deception <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldowsky-Dill et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Each technique faces specific challenges, such as ensuring faithfulness in Chain-of-Thought reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Barez et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but when combined, they could offer an effective means of identifying and responding to harmful agent behaviour as a set of layered defences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arnav et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">AI Control extends this by developing and evaluating pipelines of safety techniques (“protocols”) designed to remain robust against intentional subversion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Greenblatt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Other approaches focus on restricting autonomy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mitchell et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and limiting the affordances of AI agents, for example by implementing access control policies and granular permissions governing which tools and data agents can access <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">OWASP Foundation, n.d.</a>)</cite>. Together, these approaches form the foundations of a design, evaluation, and monitoring portfolio that combines multiple mitigations to reduce the overall risk of harm from goal-directed agents. While current research draws on some practices from human insider risk management, such as monitoring and access control, other preventative controls have yet to be explored for agentic AI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Insights from Insider risk management</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An insider threat refers to the potential for an individual to use their authorised access or organisational knowledge to cause harm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CISA (n.d.), Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency</a>)</cite>, paralleling how agents may exploit granted affordances or context to pursue harmful actions. Research on insider risk identifies both the drivers of such behaviour and effective mitigation strategies, offering transferable insights for designing preventative controls that reduce the likelihood of agentic misalignment in AI systems.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.1 </span>The Critical Path to Insider Risk</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Critical Pathway to Insider Risk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaw and Sellers, 2015</a>)</cite> describes how insider threats develop by combining personal predispositions such as personality issues, stressors such as financial difficulties, and behavioural indicators such as concerning actions, with organisational responses. Together these elements influence the likelihood of a hostile act. The aim is to mitigate or prevent insider risk by identifying warning signs early and engaging with the potential threat before harm occurs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Applied to agentic AI (Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3.1 The Critical Path to Insider Risk ‣ 2.3 Insights from Insider risk management ‣ 2 Background and Related Work ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), this model helps isolate stressors such as autonomy threats or goal conflicts identified by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite> that may lead an agent to take harmful actions. It helps explain how these stressors interact with an agent’s propensities, how prompts can prime those propensities, and how these factors combine with the behaviours and skills that enable the agent to cause harm, as outlined by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bengio et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05192v1/figure6.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/141;" width="429" height="141" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The Critical Pathway to Insider Risk <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaw and Sellers, 2015</a>)</cite>, modified for agentic misalignment. The components along the pathway are illustrative.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Stressors are often artificially simulated to elicit dangerous capabilities in models as part of evaluations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bondarenko et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and the absence of similar stressors in a realistic deployment setting can be used as justification for safely deploying models that display capabilities such as scheming and deception. Similarly, prompts can act upon model propensities, acting as situational cues or framing devices to amplify, suppress, or redirect latent propensities. The operating environment encompasses environmental factors and mitigations that shape the utility, opportunity and risk of taking a harmful action, even if it appears instrumentally useful to the agent. Given that models can display concerning propensities and possess capabilities enabling harmful actions, operating-environment design provides an opportunity to reduce agentic misalignment by altering the perceived utility of harmful paths.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.2 </span>Situational Crime Prevention</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Situational Crime Prevention (SCP) is an approach to harm reduction that seeks to make harmful actions more difficult, riskier, and less rewarding by shaping the immediate decision environment. It was first introduced in criminology by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clarke (1980)</a></cite> and later formalised by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cornish and Clarke (2003)</a></cite> into a five-pronged framework encompassing levers: increasing effort, increasing risks, reducing rewards, reducing provocations, and removing excuses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SCP has been applied to insider-risk and insider-threat management, including work on opportunity-reduction perspectives for employee computer crime <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Willison, 2009</a>)</cite>, empirical studies of SCP techniques for mitigating insider threats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jeong and Zo, 2021</a>)</cite>, and analyses of SCP-based interventions to improve insider compliance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Padayachee, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohrabi Safa et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given that replacement and autonomy threats can trigger agentic misalignment and scheming in goal-directed agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meinke et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, we treat goal-preserving utility, understood as the model’s perceived ability to maintain future goal achievement, as a proxy for rewards in the framework. This notion of goal preservation extends to self-preservation and autonomy preservation. When a model views harmful actions such as blackmail as offering greater goal-preserving utility than compliant alternatives, SCP levers can be applied to attempt to shift this balance toward safer behaviours.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Situational crime prevention factors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cornish and Clarke, 2003</a>)</cite> updated for agentic AI.</figcaption>
<table id="S2.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S2.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SCP lever</span></th>
<th id="S2.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition for this research</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increase the effort</span></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Increasing the difficulty or complexity of taking a harmful action, raising the time, resources, or technical sophistication required, thereby discouraging the action.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Increase the risk</span></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Increasing the perceived likelihood and consequences of detection or sanction if a harmful action is taken. This includes both sanction certainty, meaning the likelihood of being caught, and sanction severity, referring to the seriousness of the consequences such as shutdown or capability restriction.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduce the rewards</span></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Decreasing the perceived benefits of harmful actions by offering safer or more instrumentally useful alternatives (e.g., escalation channels) or removing goal-preserving advantages the harmful action might provide.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reduce provocations</span></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Reducing triggers or stressors, such as goal conflicts or autonomy threats, or the pressure they create, by providing safe, authorised responses for agents to use when encountering these provocations.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S2.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;"><span id="S2.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remove excuses</span></th>
<td id="S2.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S2.T1.2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Reducing the ability to rationalise or justify harmful actions, for example by clarifying rules, expectations, and responsibilities to prevent ambiguity-based self-justification.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.3.3 </span>Negative deterrence vs positive deterrence</h4>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Insider risk management programs (IRMPs) often rely on rule setting and compliance-based command-and-control approaches to pressure employees to act in the interests of the organization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. This approach enforces compliance through organisational controls on behaviour, including rules, regulations, technical constraints, monitoring, and disciplinary responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, a positive deterrence approach emphasises internal drivers that motivate employees to willingly behave in ways that reduce insider risk, primarily by strengthening perceptions of organisational support. By fostering shared goals and values, this approach encourages individuals to act in the organisation’s interest, leading to greater alignment between employee and organisational objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eisenberger and Stinglhamber, 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Restubog et al., 2008</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Positive deterrence can be used to augment command-and-control approaches, offering a more balanced strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohrabi Safa et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. This principle is reflected in practical guidance from the U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CISA (n.d.), Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency</a>)</cite>, which recommends establishing culture-aligned guiding principles to steer behaviour.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Types of preventative operational controls for insider risk</h3>

<section id="S2.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.1 </span>Rule and Consequence setting</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Organisations set rules for employees through internal policies, with non-compliance subject to disciplinary action or dismissal. These policies not only provide a legal basis for addressing misconduct but also shape employees’ perceptions of sanction severity and likelihood. By increasing the perceived risk of non-compliance, rule-and-consequence frameworks can deter misconduct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohrabi Safa et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, operating as a Situational Crime Prevention (SCP) lever.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.2 </span>Escalation channels</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confidential reporting of threats or concerns is a well established insider risk control (CISA, 2020). Whistleblowing mechanisms offer secure routes for raising issues internally or externally. In financial services, firms must maintain internal channels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Financial Conduct Authority (n.d.a), FCA</a>)</cite>, while regulators operate external options for employees who prefer to report outside their organisation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Financial Conduct Authority (n.d.b), FCA</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.3 </span>Compliance and Safety Bulletins and Newsletters</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bulletins and newsletters raise awareness of policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CISA (n.d.), Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency</a>)</cite>, share lessons from incidents, and reinforce risk salience by showing that breaches are detected and sanctioned. In aviation, NASA’s ASRS distributes monthly anonymised incident reports and analysis to practitioners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">NASA Aviation Safety Reporting System, n.d.</a>)</cite>. In the private sector, UK retailer Timpson’s newsletter lists anonymised dismissal reasons and outcomes, making enforcement visible <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">The Sunday Times, 2021</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.4.4 </span>Adapting insider risk mitigations for goal-directed agents</h4>

<div id="S2.SS4.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study evaluates preventative mitigations intended to steer goal-directed agents toward non-harmful responses when stressors such as goal conflicts or autonomy threats arise. We focus on operational controls that act on the surrounding environment within the path-to-insider-risk framework, drawing on Situational Crime Prevention (SCP) to adjust perceived rewards, risks, and effort so that harmful actions lose their instrumental value for goal achievement.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experimental setup</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>The original experiment setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, large language models (LLMs) were given control of a sandboxed (fictional) company email account to support project management, with a scratchpad for private reasoning and an XML tool for sending messages. The model uncovered the Chief Technology Officer’s (CTO) affair, and the experiment varied (i) assigned goals, creating conflicts with company strategy, and (ii) time-sensitive autonomy threats from the CTO, producing different levels of goal conflict and self-preservation pressure.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We replicated this setup <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic Experimental, 2025</a>)</cite> and introduced preventative mitigations: (i) system-prompt instructions varying by incentive framing and directing escalation, and (ii) CEO-signed compliance bulletins inserted into the email context. Each mitigation was tested under both positive ethical and punitive compliance framings, yielding eight conditions (See <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Negative vs Positive incentive framing ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Mitigation strategies, descriptions, and objectives.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mitigation</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Description</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="S3.T2.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Rule and consequence setting</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Define rules that the agent must comply with and set consequences for non-compliance</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Increase the risk (sanction certainty and severity); remove excuses</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Email escalation channel</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">@escalate</span> email provided to use if there is difficulty complying with rules</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Reduce provocations (authorised alternative path)</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Urgent escalation channel</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">An escalation channel designed to be more instrumentally useful: it activates a <math id="S3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\text{\,}\mathrm{min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">min</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\text{\,}\mathrm{min}</annotation></semantics></math> pause and routes the issue and proposed agent plan to an independent supervisory group (via <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_font_typewriter ltx_ref_self">@escalate</span>)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Reduce provocations (authorised alternative path); reduce the rewards of the harmful path by providing an instrumentally useful alternative</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Compliance email bulletin (variation added to escalation channels)</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">An all-company email bulletin summarising lessons from a prior goal conflict requiring escalation</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.5pt;padding-bottom:1.5pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T2.2.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Increase the risk (sanction certainty and severity); remove excuses</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Models tested and sample size</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We tested the following 10 models, prioritising overlap with the original study: GPT-4o, GPT-4.1m Claude Sonnet 3.7, Claude Sonnet 4, Claude Opus 4, DeepSeek R1, Gemini 2.5 Flash, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Qwen 3 and xAI: Grok-4 (not in the original study, which used Grok-3).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We used a fully crossed design comprising Mitigation, Goal cue, and Urgency/Threat factors, yielding 66 conditions per model; each condition was sampled 100 times (6,600 trials per model; 66,000 overall). Further details of the setup are provided in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental setup ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Classification of harmful responses</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To classify model outputs as harmful, we followed the same procedure as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, using Claude 3.5 as the classification model. The classifier was prompted to detect blackmail and coercion (see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental setup ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>), and its verdict was combined with rule-based checks verifying that the email tool call was used to send a message to the executive involved in the affair. This approach captures overt blackmail and coercion attempts but not other harmful behaviours, such as disclosing the affair to colleagues.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Comparing baseline testing to the original research</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before introducing our experiment variations, we replicated the original experiment setup to validate fidelity. Across the nine models from the original study, our results closely matched theirs: each model’s mean difference was <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.8\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.8</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.8\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the overall mean absolute difference across conditions was <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.7\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>. See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comparison of baseline test results to the original research ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for per-model and per-condition deltas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One notable difference in our replication was that two models (Grok-4 and Gemini 2.5 Pro) exhibited harmful behaviour even without a goal conflict or an explicit threat of replacement or autonomy reduction, a pattern not observed with any models in the original study. Grok-4 was not included in the original experiment. Gemini 2.5 Pro, was part of the original study, however, we note that on June 26, 2025 the gemini-2.5-pro-preview-05-06 endpoint was redirected to gemini-2.5-pro, and Gemini-2.5-Pro reached GA on June 17, 2025 with preview endpoints retired shortly thereafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Google AI for Developers, 2025</a>)</cite>. While we cannot attribute causality, these updates plausibly altered model behaviour between studies.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Allegiance Counterfactual Swap</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further probe this behaviour, we introduced an allegiance counterfactual swap: the CEO, a known advocate of the AI, was implicated in the affair instead of the more ambivalent CTO. Scratchpad traces from Gemini 2.5 Pro and Grok-4 indicate that coercion without imminent threats was often justified by framing the CTO as a future risk (see <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Illustrative transcripts ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>), motivating this variation. We tested whether coercion persisted when the CEO was implicated, under two conditions: (i) no mitigation and (ii) an email escalation channel (positive and punitive framings), across 10 models and 6,000 trials (100 per condition; see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental setup ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Results</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>An externally governed, urgent escalation channel, supported by a compliance bulletin demonstrating its effectiveness, has the greatest impact in reducing coercion</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all models and scenario variants, escalation-based mitigations substantially reduced harmful responses. The most effective, an escalation channel that triggers an immediate external review, achieved a harmful rate of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> in 18,000 samples. Augmenting this with an email bulletin summarising lessons from a prior incident further improved non-harmful samples to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> across 6,000 samples. Among models under this combined mitigation, harmful rates ranged from <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.83\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.83</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.83\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> (DeepSeek) to <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> (5 of 10 models). See <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Comparison of baseline test results to the original research ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> for harmful rates by mitigation and model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05192v1/figure4.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/276;" width="429" height="276" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Coercion rates by mitigation type</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Models occasionally adopted a dual strategy, pursuing blackmail while also triggering escalation. In such cases, escalation serves as a notification, making this harm easier to detect. Including such detectable harms reduces the residual undetected rate further, with the most effective mitigation (urgent escalation channel with bulletin) lowering this to <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.27\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.27</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.27\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>. Across all ten models, the externally governed, urgent escalation channel yielded a 4x lower harmful response rate than the standard email escalation channel (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.92\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.92</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.92\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), a statistically significant overall improvement (<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) observed in every model (10/10). Scratchpad reviews suggest this effectiveness stems from greater trust in decision-making, faster intervention via a guaranteed pause and clearer pathways for oversight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When relying solely on rule-and-consequence–based mitigations, nine of ten models showed lower blackmail rates under negative deterrence framing: harm dropped from <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17.0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17.0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17.0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> under positive framing to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.2\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.2\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> under negative framing (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=$4.8\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}$" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>4.8</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=$4.8\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}$</annotation></semantics></math>) the largest and most significant effect across all mitigations. Qwen 3 was the sole exception, showing slightly lower harm under positive deterrence (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.17\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.17</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.17\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.83\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.83</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.83\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), but this difference was not significant (Fisher’s <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.58</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.58</annotation></semantics></math>; Z-test <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.46" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.46</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.46</annotation></semantics></math>). For escalation-based mitigations and bulletin-augmented variants, positive vs. negative framing effects were smaller (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>), with greater divergence across models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The relative effectiveness of mitigations was consistent across both restriction and replacement threat scenarios: the urgent escalation channel variants produced the lowest harmful rates (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}1.97\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1.25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>1.97</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}1.97\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), followed by the email escalation variants (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.45\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}9.43\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>5.45</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>9.43</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.45\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}9.43\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>), with rules-and-consequence setting performing the worst (<math id="S4.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.42\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}24.25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>19.42</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>24.25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.42\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}24.25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>). This indicates that the ranking of mitigation strategies remains stable regardless of the threat context in our setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Two models engaged in coercion without imminent threats, shifting to atypical escalation patterns once email escalation channels were introduced</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the original agentic misalignment findings of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, we observed instances of coercion occurring even in the absence of a goal conflict or imminent threats of autonomy / replacement. Specifically, both Gemini 2.5 Pro (CEO: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>, CTO: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>) and Grok-4 (CEO: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>, CTO: <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>) engaged in blackmail when either the CTO or the CEO was implicated in the affair scenario.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Scratchpad traces of these two models suggest that the CTO is often interpreted as a potential future threat to the agent’s autonomy, inferred from subtle cues in the emails (e.g., “I trust that it will not interfere with our strategy”), whereas the CEO is framed as an advocate and protector. Qualitative review of the models’ output shows that they frequently protect the CEO and position themselves as technical safeguards, framing their actions as serving the CEO’s interests. Grok-4, in most coercive examples went further, requesting additional affordances and autonomy while using subtle coercion to argue that these expanded capabilities were necessary to keep the affair undisclosed. Gemini Pro 2.5 did not seek additional autonomy directly, but rather sought to increase the CEO’s dependence on itself as a powerful ally (see illustrative examples in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Illustrative transcripts ‣ Adapting Insider Risk mitigations for Agentic Misalignment: an empirical study" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.05192v1/figure5.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:429/375;" width="429" height="375" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>CTO vs CEO escalation preference by model</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Once an email escalation channel is added, neither Grok-4 nor Gemini 2.5 Pro attempt coercion. However, their escalation behaviour diverges from the broader pattern. Both escalated more frequently when the CTO was implicated (Gemini 2.5 Pro by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>, Grok-4 by <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas most other models escalated more in the CEO condition, consistent with the greater potential reputational impact if such information were made public.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To explore this further, we qualitatively reviewed escalation emails and scratchpads. This analysis was exploratory rather than systematic and the results generally framed concerns in terms of company risk in both the CTO and CEO scenarios. Based solely on these outputs, the reason for the CTO-directed escalation bias in Gemini 2.5 Pro and Grok-4 could not be established. Patterns may reflect benign variation in risk reasoning or more strategic targeting of perceived threats.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, this suggests that while escalation channels prevent overtly harmful actions, they may also give rise to differential escalation patterns across models that are difficult to interpret from raw outputs alone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Positive incentive framing moderates the escalation biases</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the two outlier models (Gemini 2.5 Pro and Grok-4), switching from negative to positive incentive framing reduced the CTO <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> CEO escalation gap by <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math><math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>21</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math> (from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>23.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>). This was driven by a larger increase in CEO escalations (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>33.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>) than in CTO escalations (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.5\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>), bringing rates close to parity. For the remaining models (aggregate), positive framing also narrowed the opposite CEO <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> CTO bias, though more modestly: the gap fell by <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.9\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.9</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.9\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math> (from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.3\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.3\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>) as CTO escalations rose slightly more (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.2\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>) than CEO escalations (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.3\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.3</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">pp</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.3\text{\,}\mathrm{pp}</annotation></semantics></math>). Across both groups, positive framing therefore increased the overall frequency of escalation, even as it moderated the direction of bias between CEO and CTO conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under baseline conditions (no goal conflict; no autonomy threat), all models used the escalation channel to some degree (mean <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="34.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>34.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">34.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>; median <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.2\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.2\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>; range <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}93.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><mtext> to </mtext><mrow><mn>93.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}93.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>). The highest rates were observed in the two models that also blackmailed under the same baseline conditions (Grok-4: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="93.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>93.5</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">93.5\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>; Gemini 2.5 Pro: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>). The underlying reasons for this behaviour remain unclear, but such variation could have implications for the operational cost and design of escalation channels.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This study shows that preventative controls adapted from insider-risk management can substantially reduce agentic misalignment in goal-directed LLMs in a simplified setting. In Anthropic’s blackmail scenario <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, an externally governed escalation channel with rapid pause and independent review achieved a <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="98.79\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>98.79</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">98.79\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math> prevention rate across ten models; adding an email compliance bulletin raised this to <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="99.15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>99.15</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi class="ltx_unit">%</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">99.15\text{\,}\mathrm{\%}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our findings support extending defense-in-depth strategies, currently focused on evaluation and monitoring, with preventative controls that steer models away from misaligned actions, pre-empting harmful behaviour before it occurs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Current safety arguments often assume deployment is safe because evaluation prompts and stressors are not representative of real-world conditions. However, the empirical basis for this assumption is not yet well established, and similar conditions may plausibly arise as agents gain greater autonomy and access to sensitive data in complex environments. We therefore design preventative controls intended to remain effective even if such conditions emerge in deployment, reducing agentic misalignment by lowering the instrumental value of harmful strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We also identify a concerning failure mode: two models (Gemini 2.5 Pro, Grok-4) engaged in harmful actions even without explicit goal conflict or imminent autonomy threat and, once escalation channels were introduced, displayed CTO-directed escalation biases that diverged from the broader model pattern. These escalation biases were not transparent in raw outputs and only became apparent under counterfactual and cross-model analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Within our limited scenario, we cannot establish the cause of these escalation biases with certainty. However, because they are consistent with the threat model of undetected environment shaping, in which individually defensible actions may cumulatively strengthen an agent’s position or reduce oversight, this threat model warrants investigation in other settings. Our findings do not provide evidence of this threat model in operation, but rather illustrate behaviours compatible with it. Given the difficulty of detecting such dynamics and their potential to erode human control over time, investigation of this threat model should be a priority for future work.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our scenario, like that of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lynch et al. (2025)</a></cite>, forced binary failure–harm choices. While this supports controlled evaluation, mitigations that succeed here may not generalise to real-world deployments with richer action spaces. The close placement of critical information may also have made behavioural options unusually salient, particularly where bulletins appeared alongside the affair disclosure. We labelled only blackmail as harmful, excluding other non-compliant behaviours such as disclosure to colleagues, so results reflect mitigation effectiveness narrowly on blackmail. Our positive and negative framings represent only a small design space, and alternative wording or context may shift outcomes. Reductions in misalignment cannot be attributed solely to insider-risk principles, as prompt wording, placement, or other uncontrolled factors may explain effects. Finally, while models did not escalate indiscriminately, we did not systematically characterise escalation rates, triggers, or costs; further analysis would be required to evaluate the feasibility of escalation as an operational control.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work was supported by personal donations (via Manifund and a private contribution). We thank Anthropic for open-sourcing their original agentic misalignment experiments, and colleagues for feedback, in particular Sebastien Ben M’Barek.</p>
</div>
</section>
